  "faq": [
    {
      "question": "What is the best time to visit Tromsø for Northern Lights?",
      "answer": "The best time to see the Northern Lights in Tromsø is between late September and late March. Peak season typically runs from November to February, when the nights are longest and darkest, maximizing your chances. However, sightings are never guaranteed and depend on solar activity and clear skies."
    },
    {
      "question": "What should I wear on a Northern Lights tour?",
      "answer": "Layers are key! Wear thermal base layers (wool or synthetic), a warm mid-layer (fleece or down), and a waterproof, windproof outer shell. Don't forget a warm hat that covers your ears, thick gloves or mittens, a scarf, and insulated, waterproof winter boots with thick socks. Many tours provide extra warm suits."
    },
    {
      "question": "How likely is it to see the Northern Lights?",
      "answer": "Tromsø is located in the heart of the aurora oval, making it one of the best places in the world to see the Northern Lights. While sightings are never 100% guaranteed, many tour operators boast a high success rate (often over 80-90% over a week) due to their ability to chase clear skies. Staying for several nights and booking multiple tours increases your chances."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I see the Northern Lights from Tromsø city center?",
      "answer": "It is possible to see faint Northern Lights from Tromsø city center if the aurora activity is very strong and the skies are clear. However, city light pollution significantly diminishes the visibility and intensity. For the best and most vibrant displays, it's highly recommended to join a tour that takes you away from the city lights into the darker surrounding wilderness."
    },
    {
      "question": "What's the difference between a small group and a big bus Northern Lights tour?",
      "answer": "Small group tours (typically 8-16 people) offer a more personalized experience, often with expert guides who provide photography assistance, campfire opportunities, and more flexibility in choosing viewing spots. Big bus tours (20-50+ people) are generally more budget-friendly but offer less personal interaction and flexibility. Both aim to find clear skies, but the experience differs significantly."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do I need special camera equipment to photograph the Northern Lights?",
      "answer": "While a smartphone can capture some faint aurora, for truly stunning photos, a DSLR or mirrorless camera with manual settings, a wide-angle lens (f/2.8 or wider), and a sturdy tripod are highly recommended. Many tours offer tripods, and guides can help with settings, but having your own equipment and knowing its basics will yield the best results."
    }
